    deepLore: `The eighth day witnessed a profound emotional and spiritual tragedy for Arjuna. His brave son Iravan (born to the Naga princess Ulupi) led a devastating charge against the Kaurava cavalry. Iravan fought with breathtaking valor, single-handedly slaying five of Shakuni's brothers and decimating the Gandhara forces.\n\nPanicked by Iravan's unstoppable rampage, Duryodhana summoned the terrifying Rakshasa (demon) Alambusha, who harbored a blood-feud against Arjuna. A spectacular magical battle erupted between Iravan (using Naga magic) and Alambusha (using Rakshasa illusions). Iravan transformed into a colossal serpent to crush the demon, but Alambusha transformed into a massive eagle, tearing Iravan's serpent form apart and ultimately severing the young hero's head.\n\n[In rich Tamil and South Indian traditions (specifically the Koothandavar cult), Iravan's story is vastly expanded. Before the war even began, Lord Krishna declared that a flawless warrior must be offered as a human sacrifice (Kalappali) to Goddess Kali to ensure Pandava victory. Iravan volunteered, but asked for one final boon: to experience marriage before dying. Since no woman would marry a man doomed to die the next morning, Lord Krishna transformed Himself into the beautiful enchantress Mohini, married Iravan, spent the night with him, and then mourned his death as a widow. This myth is celebrated annually in the magnificent Koovagam festival].\n\nWhen Arjuna heard of his son's death, he collapsed in grief. Enraged by the murder of his nephew, Ghatotkacha unleashed a wave of unholy terror. He summoned a literal storm of blood, raining rocks and weapons down upon the Kaurava army. His horrific illusions shattered the Kaurava flanks, forcing them to break formation and flee in terror before nightfall finally halted the slaughter.`,
