    summary: "Carried behind Shikhandi on Arjuna's chariot, Bhishma refuses to raise his weapons. Pierced by a countless network of arrows shot by Arjuna, the grand patriarch of the Kurus falls, creating a bed of arrows that cushions his body above the earth.",
    deepLore: `When dawn broke on the tenth day, the battlefield felt unnaturally still. Arjuna rode to the absolute front lines, but he did not stand alone. Positioned prominently at the front of his chariot was Shikhandi. \n\nAs the chariot approached the Kaurava center, Bhishma raised his mighty bow to strike. But his eyes fell upon Shikhandi. Through divine vision, Bhishma saw the burning, vengeful spirit of Princess Amba layered over Shikhandi's face. True to his sacred vow of chivalry, Bhishma immediately lowered his bow, smiled peacefully, and refused to fight.\n\nCapitalizing on this moral shield, Shikhandi fired a barrage of arrows, but they barely scratched Bhishma's celestial armor. From behind Shikhandi, weeping bitter tears, Arjuna drew the Gandiva bow. He unleashed a full, unhindered torrent of armor-piercing arrows. The arrows struck Bhishma with devastating force, piercing his chest, his arms, and his legs. A dense, interlocking web of celestial arrows penetrated every inch of the patriarch's body.\n\nFinally, the mighty Grandfather Bhishma collapsed from his chariot. However, because his body was pierced by hundreds of long arrows, he did not touch the blood-soaked earth. Instead, the shafts formed a comfortable, elevated bed of arrows (Sharashayya) that kept his body suspended in the air. \n\nPossessing the divine boon of 'Ichha Mrityu' (the ability to choose the exact time of his death), Bhishma refused to let his soul leave his body, choosing to endure the agonizing pain for 58 days until the sun moved into the auspicious northern hemisphere (Uttarayana). \n\nCombat instantly ceased. Warriors from both sides, throwing down their weapons in grief, walked reverently to the fallen hero to pay their respects. Noting that his head was hanging uncomfortably, Bhishma asked for a pillow. While Duryodhana rushed to bring silk pillows, Bhishma rejected them. Arjuna understood; he shot three arrows deep into the earth to perfectly support Bhishma's head. When Bhishma asked for water, Arjuna fired the Parjanyastra (Rain weapon) into the ground beside him, conjuring a cool, pure underground stream of Mother Ganga's water that shot straight into Bhishma's lips, quenching the dying patriarch's thirst. The first, grandest pillar of the Kaurava army had fallen.`,
